## 6. Narrative Spine

### 6.1 The intellectual spine (one paragraph)

Governance instruments for AI are not best understood through net-effects models that ask whether more transparency, or stronger accountability, or wider stakeholder inclusion *individually* produces legitimate AI governance. They are best understood **configurationally**: it is the *combination* of ethical alignment (EA), stakeholder role clarity (SR), regulatory logic (RL), and stakeholder inclusion (SI) — and the absence or presence of each in conjunction with the others — that determines whether a governance regime achieves the trustworthiness outcomes its drafters claim. Building on the 49-instrument PRISMA corpus and the 3×3 archetype typology established in the precursor typology work (which decomposed governance instruments at the rule level using Institutional Grammar 2.0), this chapter selects 10 cases for a small-N fuzzy-set Qualitative Comparative Analysis (fsQCA) and asks: **which configurations of EA × SR × RL × SI are sufficient for legitimate AI governance outcomes, and how do those configurations differ across jurisdictional, sectoral, and maturity contexts?**

### 6.2 Four-condition model

| Condition | Source in typology work | Calibration anchor (high; 1.0) | Calibration anchor (low; 0.0) |
|---|---|---|---|
| **EA — Ethical Alignment** | Interpretive overlay in GIQ_V13; promoted to causal condition here | Mandated algorithmic explainability + published documentation (per Table S4) | No disclosure mechanisms |
| **SR — Stakeholder Role Clarity** | Typology axis in GIQ_V13 | Multi-tier grievance redressal + defined liability across actors | Absent or implicit role assignment |
| **RL — Regulatory Logic** | Typology axis in GIQ_V13 | Risk-tiered with binding obligations and clear deontics | Voluntary, advisory, or VOID or-else |
| **SI — Stakeholder Inclusion** | Typology axis in GIQ_V13 | Mandatory consultation, civil society participation, ex post review | No participation mechanism |

### 6.3 The methodological bridge (D2 — must be made explicit in the chapter)

The three typology axes (RL, SR, SI) become three of the four fsQCA conditions directly. EA, which functions in the typology paper as an *interpretive overlay* on the nine archetypes (used to read the politics of each archetype), is **promoted** in this chapter to a fourth causal condition. The justification is methodological: the typology paper's question is descriptive ("what archetypes exist?"); a descriptive answer can hold EA as an overlay because it is asking what shapes the rules take. The chapter's question is causal ("what is sufficient for the outcome?"); a causal answer needs EA *inside* the configuration because EA is part of what produces (or fails to produce) the outcome.

### 6.4 The chapter contribution claim (one sentence)

> *Across 10 jurisdictionally and sectorally varied AI governance regimes, no single condition is necessary for legitimate AI governance outcomes; instead, two equifinal configurational paths are sufficient — one anchored in binding regulatory logic with high stakeholder role clarity (the "enforcement-first" path, characteristic of advanced jurisdictions), and one anchored in high ethical alignment combined with high stakeholder inclusion even where regulatory logic is non-binding (the "legitimacy-first" path, characteristic of emerging jurisdictions exercising soft-law authority) — with implications for how regulatory design choices in differently-resourced jurisdictions can converge on equivalent legitimacy outcomes through non-equivalent configurational routes.*

(This is a **placeholder claim** that the actual analysis must validate, falsify, or refine. It is included here as the working narrative spine so that all chapter sections can be drafted with a clear endpoint in mind. The state file must record that the empirical analysis has not yet been conducted and that this claim may be revised.)

